S=[
('Confidence comes from a comprehensible action','A synthesis of 144studies found competence was the strongest predictor of self-determined student motivation.','Offer one attainable decision, then explain the exact winning feature. Keep an experienced-user exit.','Educational evidence; no causal onboarding or retention result for Glass Table.','Bureau et al.,2022','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35330866/'),
('Show the rule before asking','Five algebra experiments studied worked examples as an alternative to unguided novice problem solving.','First explain two equal ranks as a pair; only then ask which hand wins. Cards carry the explanation.','Algebra experiments; experts may need less instruction.','Sweller & Cooper,1985','https://doi.org/10.1207/s1532690xci0201_3'),
('A choice can teach; a guess is not mastery','Retrieval improved delayed retention of prose, although repeated study performed better immediately.','After a taught example, use a changed deal. Explain correct answers too. Reserve mastery claims for stronger evidence.','Memory tasks, not mobile engagement; a single multiple-choice answer is weak evidence.','Roediger & Karpicke,2006','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16507066/'),
('Feedback needs useful information','A feedback meta-analysis included435studies and found differing effects across feedback types.','Name what to look at and why it wins. Give a next action. Wrong and unsure are different states.','Learning feedback is heterogeneous; generic praise is not equivalent to explanation.','Wisniewski et al.,2020','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32038429/'),
('Make progress visible and accurate','Across138randomized studies, monitoring progress improved goal attainment on average (d=.40).','Show2examples completed and the rules explored; later show comparable practice counts and a small activity graphic.','Goal attainment across domains, not proof that a streak increases app retention.','Harkin et al.,2016','https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/91437/'),
('Use praise that earns trust','Praise can help, hurt or have no effect; sincerity, autonomy, competence and social comparison matter.','Use “That’s right—the pair decides this hand.” Avoid identity labels and praise for inferred effort.','Primarily child research; adult Korean/English tone must be tested.','Henderlong & Lepper,2002','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12206194/'),
('Badges are not a substitute for a useful lesson','A five-week study-app investigation found greater autonomous motivation with gamification, but not greater app-use duration/frequency or performance.','Make the practice satisfying first; keep the already-approved quiet activity indicator optional and secondary.','One student app/context; not a general verdict against all gamification.','Baars et al.,2022','https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.793002/full'),
('Returning should feel easy','A study of96volunteers repeating everyday behaviors found substantial variation in habit formation; one missed opportunity did not materially change the process.','Offer a small repeatable practice and kind re-entry. Keep earned progress when people miss days.','Food/drink/activity behaviors; no universal21-day app habit promise.','Lally et al.,2010','https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674'),
('Protect the thought in progress','Phone notifications impaired an attention-demanding laboratory task even without interacting with the phone.','Keep the explanation present until Next. Avoid reward interruptions, auto-advance and notification prompts in the opening.','Does not measure a universal human attention span.','Stothart et al.,2015','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26121498/'),
('Teach through the interface','Apple recommends onboarding that is fast, fun and optional, with instruction beside what it describes.','A small card comparison, visible help and a clear exit fit the product better than a long introductory speech.','Platform guidance; not psychological outcome evidence.','Apple onboarding','https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/onboarding')]
css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f4f1ea;color:#222c29;font:16px/1.65 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Segoe UI,sans-serif}main{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:40px 28px}h1{font-size:clamp(32px,5vw,54px);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.035em;margin:14px 0 24px;max-width:900px}h2{font-size:30px;line-height:1.2;margin:44 px 0 20px}h3{font-size:20px;line-height:1.3}a{color:#235f50;text-underline-offset:3px}p{max-width:880px}small,.muted{color:#515e58}.eyebrow{font-size:13px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:.06em}nav{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:3;display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:14px 20px;background:#fffdf7;border-bottom:1px solid #c4bcb0}nav a{font-size:14px}select,button{font:inherit;border:1px solid #746e65;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;background:#fffdf7;color:#222c29}button{cursor:pointer}a:focus-visible,button:focus-visible,select:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #397565;outline-offset:3px}.lead{font-size:21px;max-width:820px}.callout{padding:24px;border-left:4px solid #8a5500;background:#fffdf7;margin:24px 0}.cols,.pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:28px}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px}.finding{padding:22px;background:#fffdf7;border:1px solid #d6cfc3;border-radius:14px}.finding p{font-size:15px}.finding small{display:block}.finding h3{margin:0 0 14px}.badge{font-size:12px;color:#515e58;font-weight:600}.table-wrap{overflow-x:auto}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px}td,th{border-bottom:1px solid #c4bcb0;padding:12px;vertical-align:top;text-align:left}th{font-weight:650}figure{margin:0}figcaption{font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#515e58;margin:10px 0 20px}.shot{display:block;width:100%;max-width:320px;height:auto;border-radius:14px;border:1px solid #c4bcb0;cursor:zoom-in}.gallery{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:24px}.filter{display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:18px 0}.filter label{display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:center}.route{padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #c4bcb0}.route h3{margin-top:0}.route ul{padding-left:20px}.route a.cta{display:inline-block;padding:10px 18px;border-radius:10px;background:#8a5500;color:white;text-decoration:none}.flow{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px;align-items:center}.flow span{padding:10px 14px;background:#fffdf7;border:1px solid #c4bcb0;border-radius:9px}.timeline{border-left:2px solid #c4bcb0;padding-left:24px}.timeline p{position:relative}.timeline p:before{content:'';position:absolute;left:-31px;top:8px;width:12px;height:12px;border-radius:50%;background:#235f50}.swatch{display:inline-block;width:24px;height:24px;border-radius:5px;vertical-align:middle;border:1px solid #746e65;margin-right:8px}.checklist li{margin-bottom:12px}footer{margin-top:44 px;border-top:1px solid #c4bcb0;padding-top:22px;font-size:14px}details{padding:16px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650}@media(max-width:700px){main{padding:24px 18px}.cols,.cards{grid-template-columns:1fr}.pair{gap:12px}.gallery{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:12px}nav{position:relative;gap:12px}h2{font-size:26px}.finding{padding:18px}.lead{font-size:18px}.shot{border-radius:9px}.filter{gap:10px}}'''
p=['<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><link rel="icon" href="data:,"><title>Glass Table · the first five minutes</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body><nav><a href="#recommendation">Recommendation</a><a href="#before">Before / after</a><a href="#routes">Try three openings</a><a href="#research">Research</a><a href="#craft">Buttons & motion</a><a href="#validation">Testing</a><a href="#history">Build history</a></nav><main><div class="eyebrow">GLASS TABLE / OCTOBER 10, 2026 / BUILD 19 FEEDBACK</div><h1>Make the first small decision feel worth taking</h1><p class="lead">Teach something useful before asking someone to describe their ability. Show the reason, notice real progress, and make the next step an invitation.</p><p>This is a research and interactive HTML exploration. The installed Swift app remains 1.0(19). Proposed screenshots are browser renders; native before screenshots have separate provenance. No build20, phone update or new learning claim is implied.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Yes, the record exists—and it is now easier to follow.</strong><p><a href="../../build-history.md">One linked build/feedback index</a> reconciles builds 1–19, including missing installation evidence. Build 19’s fresh 12mini installation and Korean welcome were verified. Your new comments are recorded as FFM01–08; they reopen onboarding quality, not the already-completed five-example bank.</p></div><h2 id="recommendation">My recommendation</h2><p><strong>Choose B: experience first.</strong> A concise welcome leads to a taught pair comparison, a one-tap decision, clear feedback, and a changed comparison. The learner gets useful evidence of what the app feels like before self-reporting. Offer familiarity later as a starting preference. Keep a “Choose a lesson” exit for experienced people.</p><p>A, the conversational familiarity question, is the smallest conceptual change and directly answers your suggested wording. C, a yes/no/unsure question, is comfortable but adds a tap without teaching or changing the route; I would not use it as the default.</p><p>The healthy repeat loop is: <strong>curiosity → understandable choice → explanation → visible small progress → voluntary next step.</strong> Success means people learn and want to return, with a comfortable stopping point. Session duration alone is not the goal.</p><h2 id="before">The abrupt transition you noticed</h2><p>Source-confirmed build19 route: welcome → Start → familiarity → (three starting-point questions for some people) → five comparisons. “New to poker” skips those three questions. Familiarity helps a recommendation; it is not a grade. The opening currently does not explain that purpose well, and its “three questions” copy does not describe the new-user route accurately.</p><div class="filter"><label>Language<select id="lang"><option value="en">English</option><option value="ko">한국어</option></select></label><label>Appearance<select id="mode"><option>light</option><option>dark</option></select></label></div><div class="pair"><figure><img class="shot dynamic" data-base="assets/before" data-scene="welcome" alt="Native build19 welcome"><figcaption>Before · Native build19 simulator welcome. Phone evidence also confirms this welcome in Korean.</figcaption></figure><figure><img class="shot proposal" data-scene="B-welcome" alt="Experience-first welcome proposal"><figcaption>After · B welcome. Name the immediate action and available help; remove the initial administrative turn.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="pair"><figure><img class="shot dynamic" data-base="assets/before" data-scene="placement" alt="Historical native familiarity screen"><figcaption>Before · Historical build16 native familiarity screen. Build 19 source retains this prompt/copy; this is not a new build19 device capture.</figcaption></figure><figure><img class="shot proposal" data-scene="A-familiarity" alt="Explained familiarity proposal"><figcaption>After · A alternative. Explain why the question is useful and that it is a preference, with a direct basics route.</figcaption></figure></div><h2 id="routes">Three openings you can actually click through</h2>']
for route,title,benefit,cost in [('B','Experience first · recommended','One rule, one easy decision, feedback, another comparison, then a factual recap. Help is a neutral choice; correct and wrong answers both explain why.','Changes the placement order and introduces a pause after two examples. The five categories remain available; full guide completion would still require its defined completion condition. Native resume/progress behavior needs a separately verified implementation.'),('A','Explain, then ask','Welcome → purpose of familiarity → three plain choices → a taught example. Keeps the current familiarity-before-example concept. The prototype defers the three diagnostic questions; a copy-only variant could retain current routing.','Still asks users to label themselves before experiencing the lesson. Experienced users need a clear bypass rather than mandatory basics.'),('C','Tiny question first','Yes / No / Not sure feels low stakes. All answers move to the same example; no knowledge score is inferred.','“Have you seen poker?” measures exposure, not understanding. Because it does not change the lesson, it adds an unnecessary interaction; retain only if user testing supports it.')]:
 p+=['<article class="route"><h3>'+title+'</h3><div class="cols"><p>'+benefit+'</p><p class="muted">Tradeoff: '+cost+'</p></div><a class="cta" href="prototype.html?route='+route+'">Try opening '+route+'</a></article>']
p+=['<p>The prototypes include Previous, Close, correct, wrong, “Show me first,” remaining trips/straight/flush comparisons, a stop state, language/appearance controls, button/palette trials and large text. Local prototype state is not saved to the app. Use Reset to repeat a route.</p><h2>A first-five-minute journey</h2><p>These are testing hypotheses, not a timer or a universal attention limit. Readers control pace and explanations never vanish after a fixed number of seconds.</p><div class="timeline"><p><strong>Opening:</strong> “Try one simple hand.” Show cards and one concrete promise: explanations stay available.</p><p><strong>First decision:</strong> Teach a pair beside the cards. Two answer choices plus “Show me first.” Avoid abbreviations, pot percentages and hand scores.</p><p><strong>First explanation:</strong> Outline the relevant pair only after commitment. State the rule in one or two sentences. Let the learner control Next.</p><p><strong>Changed example:</strong> Compare two pairs. Say which feature matters. Later test the first rule with a genuinely new deal; a second rule is not proof of transfer.</p><p><strong>Small checkpoint:</strong> “2 examples explored.” Show the two rules and offer the remaining 3comparisons or a comfortable stop. Exposure is not mastery.</p><p><strong>Optional continuation:</strong> A short hand together after basics, or a chosen lesson. Ask familiarity only where it helps this choice. Keep the existing chart available and help neutral.</p></div><h2>Success, mistakes and uncertainty</h2><div class="gallery">']
for scene,caption in [('A-welcome','Alternative · conversational welcome'),('B-transfer-feedback','Second explanation · higher pair'),('B-example','Teach the rule before the choice'),('B-feedback-correct','Correct · identify the feature'),('B-feedback-wrong','Wrong · explain without judgment'),('B-feedback-help','Unsure · show it, do not mark wrong'),('B-transfer','Next rule · higher pair'),('B-recap','Progress · factual and small'),('B-remaining','Continue · three remaining categories'),('B-done','Stop · useful completion without pressure'),('C-tiny','Alternative · yes/no/unsure')]:p+=['<figure><img class="shot proposal" data-scene="'+scene+'" alt="'+caption+'"><figcaption>'+caption+'</figcaption></figure>']
p+=['</div><h2 id="research">What the research supports—and what it does not</h2><p>Primary experiments, original reviews and Apple guidance were checked. Most studies concern school, memory or everyday habits. Applying them to casual poker learning is a design inference, not a measured retention result.</p><div class="cards">']
for title,evidence,apply,limit,name,url in S:p+=['<article class="finding"><h3>'+title+'</h3><p>'+evidence+' <a href="'+url+'">'+name+'</a>.</p><p><strong>Apply:</strong> '+apply+'</p><small>Limit: '+limit+'</small></article>']
p+=['</div><p>Further mechanism context: mastery experiences supported efficacy in a clinical adult experiment, which is far from poker onboarding. It suggests testing an earned first action rather than relying on praise alone. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15093/">Bandura et al.,1977</a>. <a href="../../research/2026-10-10-first-five-minutes-learning.md">Full research notes and source boundaries</a>.</p><h2>Words, tone and attention</h2><p>There is no validated sentence length that makes this app engaging. Try one idea per sentence; roughly8–16English words is an editorial starting point, not a psychological cutoff. At normal375–390pt portrait width, start with a1–2line heading and2–3line introduction, then test. Korean needs natural clauses rather than translated word counts. Normal line groups are paired; large text may reflow naturally.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Situation</th><th>English proposal</th><th>Korean proposal</th><th>Rule</th></tr>']
copy=[('Start','Try one simple hand. We’ll explain each choice.','쉬운 패 하나부터 볼까요? 선택마다 이유를 알려드릴게요.','Immediate value; no generic Start'),('Ask experience','To choose a comfortable start, how familiar is poker?','편하게 시작할 수 있도록 물어볼게요. 포커가 얼마나 익숙한가요?','Explain purpose; optional preference'),('Correct','That’s right. The two aces make a pair.','맞았어요. 에이스 두 장이 페어를 만들어요.','Praise the observed decision'),('Wrong','Look at the two aces. Together they make a pair.','에이스 두 장을 볼까요? 같은 숫자 두 장이 페어예요.','Point to evidence; no fake success'),('Unsure','Let’s look together.','함께 살펴볼게요.','Show me first; not a scored error'),('Several mistakes','Let’s use a simpler example. Keep the explanation open.','더 간단한 예시로 볼까요? 설명을 계속 열어둘 수 있어요.','Change support, not just repeat reassurance'),('Progress','2 examples explored · pairs','예시2개 살펴보기 · 페어','Count exposure, not mastery'),('Return','Welcome back. Pick up where you left off.','다시 오셨네요. 이어서 해볼까요?','Only if an actual saved activity exists'),('Stop','Finish for now','오늘은 여기까지','Neutral stopping point')]
for row in copy:p+=['<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(x)+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>']
p+=['</table></div><p>Avoid “You’re a genius,” “Don’t lose your streak,” “You’re falling behind,” or an app claiming “I win” in feedback. Choice labels may reflect the learner’s perspective; narration addresses the learner or names the hand directly. Do not infer effort, improvement, bluff intent or mastery from a correct tap.</p><h2>Show progress people can feel</h2><div class="cols"><section><h3>Preserve what is already good</h3><ul><li>Offline, no account, no money pressure.</li><li>One-tap answers, available help and optional chart.</li><li>Bottom actions and visible Previous/Close.</li><li>Quiet activity indicator, facts beside the recap, and professional tone.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Refine the meaning</h3><ul><li>Separate “explored,” “completed,” and “recalled in a new example.”</li><li>Show a compact lesson path and actual activity dots, not just text blocks.</li><li>Use improvement claims only with comparable concepts, denominators and enough evidence.</li><li>After repeated trouble, offer a simpler example or demonstration. After a break, offer a small next task.</li></ul></section></div><p>Your strongest product decisions are help freedom, simple input and wanting to explain every decision. The weak point is the transition from welcome into assessment, plus a long chain that can feel like prerequisites. A cleaner start needs learning value, not another decorative welcome page. Add only what earns its space.</p><h2 id="craft">Button, palette and motion exploration</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Current native</th><th>Trial / recommendation</th><th>Why / limits</th></tr><tr><td>Primary / secondary</td><td>54pt /50pt;14pt radius</td><td>Preserve baseline; compare52vs60pt answer rows</td><td>60is easier to hit but consumes space;52is denser. All answers use equal targets and style. Size trial is not a retention finding.</td></tr><tr><td>Tap region</td><td>≥44pt chrome targets</td><td>Keep≥44pt; visible gap between choices</td><td>Apple recommends adequate hit regions. CSSpx prototype is not native-point verification.</td></tr><tr><td>Palette</td><td>Warm neutrals, amber actions, green status</td><td>Keep amber default; explore muted teal for action only</td><td>Semantic roles, contrast and comfort matter; no universal addictive color. Correctness is never encoded only in color.</td></tr><tr><td>Press</td><td>Spring response.22s; scale.98</td><td>Preserve native; optional prototype120mspress</td><td>Immediate feedback, not attention-grabbing movement. Reduce Motion removes scaling.</td></tr><tr><td>Content transition</td><td>Spring response.34s</td><td>Try180msfade for explanation; retain native navigation direction</td><td>Timing is a trial. Explanations wait for Next; no auto-advance or looping glow.</td></tr><tr><td>Sound/haptics</td><td>Existing optional preferences</td><td>Quiet, event-based and independently disableable</td><td>Do not play punitive error sounds or reward noise on every tap. HTML does not verify physical feedback.</td></tr><tr><td>Large text</td><td>Native Dynamic Type</td><td>Prototype160/200% puts content/actions in one scroll</td><td>Browser zoom is a layout trial, not native AX5/VoiceOver evidence.</td></tr></table></div><p><a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/buttons">Apple buttons</a>, <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/color">color</a>, <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/motion">motion</a>. Proposed colors keep fixed paper cards and red/black suit ink; changing the action accent does not change the winning answer.</p><div class="gallery">']
for s,c in [('B-example-60','60px button trial'),('B-example-teal','Muted teal action trial'),('B-recap-teal','Teal continuation hierarchy'),('B-example-large','200% text · scroll to actions')]:p+=['<figure><img class="shot proposal" data-scene="'+s+'" alt="'+c+'"><figcaption>'+c+' · HTML proposal</figcaption></figure>']
p+=['</div><h2 id="validation">How to decide which opening works</h2><p>Start with small observed usability rounds:4–6adult poker beginners per language, plus a few people who already play. This is a practical recruitment target, not a statistically powered retention experiment. Give each person only one opening first; alternate A/B between participants, then use the other for preference discussion. C can be removed unless it solves an observed issue.</p><ol class="checklist"><li><strong>Can they begin without your explanation?</strong> Record hesitations, terms they cannot explain and time to a useful action. Faster is not automatically better.</li><li><strong>Did they learn the rule?</strong> Ask them to explain why the hand wins in their own words, then give a new deal with changed ranks/ownership. Recheck after24–48hours if they consent.</li><li><strong>Are wrong and unsure safe?</strong> Observe both states. Does the learner know what to inspect and how to continue? Never treat choosing help as failure.</li><li><strong>Can they stop and return?</strong> Find the exit, notice what progress actually means, and choose their own next step. Use voluntary diaries/interviews for return motivation.</li><li><strong>Can they use the controls?</strong> Test12mini, normal/large text, both languages/appearances, one-handed taps, screen readers and Reduce Motion on native implementation.</li></ol><p>Keep researcher notes with consent rather than adding telemetry to the offline app. Measure explanation and delayed transfer alongside enjoyment and voluntary return; session length, a streak and fast guesses do not prove learning. For a later quantitative comparison, calculate sample size from a defined outcome and observed baseline instead of pretending a small round proves a retention lift.</p><h3>Four areas to keep separate</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Glass Table example</th><th>Next useful action</th></tr><tr><td>Known and evidenced</td><td>Build 19 installed; welcome, native tests and source routing are recorded</td><td>Preserve provenance and track every new feedback item</td></tr><tr><td>Believed but not proved</td><td>A short first success may be more inviting than self-report</td><td>Compare A/B with first-time poker learners</td></tr><tr><td>Known gaps</td><td>Adult beginner comprehension and delayed retention are not demonstrated</td><td>Observe explanations and test a changed deal later</td></tr><tr><td>Surprises to seek</td><td>Interrupted sessions, accessibility interpretation, cultural tone and novice card conventions</td><td>Let varied participants use it without coaching</td></tr></table></div><h3>Unknowns to investigate before treating these ideas as facts</h3><p>Screen-reader interpretation of cards; older adults’ reading/touch comfort; novice distinction between rank and suit; whether English and Korean warmth land similarly; experienced users’ patience; interrupt/resume behavior; novelty wearing off after a week; accessibility transitions; and whether a correct answer was recognized or guessed. These are useful research questions, not presumed defects.</p><h2 id="history">Build and feedback history</h2><p>Historical evidence remains unchanged. The index separates implemented, tested, installed and observed. Missing records stay missing. Your current feedback belongs to installed build19, and this report remains a proposal.</p><details><summary>Show all19build records</summary><div class="table-wrap"><table><tr><th>Build/date</th><th>Main change</th><th>Evidence and limits</th></tr>']
for n,d,c,e,path in json.loads((R/'.scratch/first-five-minutes/history.json').read_text()):p+=['<tr><td>'+str(n)+' · '+d+'</td><td>'+c+'</td><td>'+e+' <a href="../../../'+path+'">Record</a></td></tr>' if path.startswith('docs/') else '<tr><td>'+str(n)+' · '+d+'</td><td>'+c+'</td><td>'+e+'</td></tr>']
p+=['</table></div></details><p><a href="../../build-history.md">Open the linked Markdown index</a> · <a href="../../../.scratch/first-five-minutes/feedback.md">Current feedback record</a> · <a href="../../../.build/native-ux-b19/index.html">Build 19 native delivery report</a></p><h2>Proposed decisions</h2><ul><li><strong>Default opening:</strong> B, experience first; A is the conservative alternative.</li><li><strong>Unknown response:</strong> “Show me first,” as neutral learning help.</li><li><strong>Familiarity:</strong> later optional preference, with a visible experienced-user lesson route.</li><li><strong>Visuals:</strong> preserve current amber/warm neutrals and native button/motion tokens until usability suggests a reason to change.</li><li><strong>Progress:</strong> factual examples/rules explored first; no new reward currency or help label.</li></ul><p>No additional owner decision is required to read or try this report. If you prefer A’s conversational opening, that is the meaningful alternative to B. Native changes follow after choosing/testing a direction; installed build19 has not changed.</p><footer><strong>Verification:</strong> <span id="verification">68 HTML proposal screenshots plus native before evidence. Browser checks: EN/KO × light/dark, matched normal line counts across 17 scenes, correct/wrong/help/Previous/Close/continuation, 44 px minimum active targets, 200% one-scroll action reachability. Report checked 390/1440 px, filters, images, links and console. These are browser checks, not native accessibility or beginner testing.</span><p>Native before: build 19 welcome and build 16 historical placement fixtures. Proposed after: this report’s HTML prototype, not Swift screenshots. Native gesture, Dynamic Type, saved progress, physical sound/haptics, novice comprehension and retention remain unverified for these proposals.</p></footer></main><script>function update(){const l=document.querySelector("#lang").value,m=document.querySelector("#mode").value;document.querySelectorAll(".dynamic").forEach(i=>i.src=i.dataset.base+"/"+l+"/"+m+"/"+i.dataset.scene+".png");document.querySelectorAll(".proposal").forEach(i=>i.src="assets/after/"+l+"/"+m+"/"+i.dataset.scene+".png")}document.querySelectorAll("select").forEach(x=>x.addEventListener("change",update));document.querySelectorAll(".shot").forEach(i=>i.addEventListener("click",()=>window.open(i.src,"_blank")));update();</script></body></html>']
